{{also|rubicon}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₁rewdʰ-}}
From {{der|en|la|Rubicō}}, {{m|la|Rubicōn||the Rubicon}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=Rubicon|pos=n|id=168363|date=March 2011|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico}}</ref> possibly from {{m|la|rubeus||red, reddish}}, from {{m|la|rubeō||to be red}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₁rewdʰ-||red}}, an allusion to the [[colour#Noun|colour]] of the river caused by [[mud]] [[deposit#Noun|deposits]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹuːbɪkɒn/|/-k(ə)n/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹubəˌkɑn/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|Ru|bi|con}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} {{place|en|small river|in northeastern|c/Italy}} which [[flow#Verb|flowed]] into the [[Adriatic Sea]] [[mark#Verb|marking]] the [[boundary]] between the [[Roman#Adjective|Roman]] [[province]] of [[Gaul]] and the Roman [[heartland]]. Its [[crossing#Noun|crossing]] by {{w|Julius Caesar}} in 49 {{B.C.E.|dots=on}} began a [[civil war]].
# The same river in the present day, identified with a river previously named Fiumicino.

